{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 37, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a prince of the Crusader state of the County of Tripoli. He was born in 1140 and died in 1187. Raymond III was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III was still a minor when his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age. \n\nAfter reaching adulthood Raymond III became involved in the politics of the Crusader states. He was a member of the Haute Cour of Jerusalem and played a significant role in the governance of the kingdom. *Reign as Count of Tripoli* Raymond III's reign as Count of Tripoli was marked by his attempts to maintain the independence of his county and to protect it from the growing threat of the Zengid dynasty. \n\nIn 1174 Raymond III was appointed regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem on behalf of King Baldwin IV who was suffering from leprosy. *Regency and Later Life* As regent Raymond III worked to strengthen the kingdom's defenses and to secure its borders. He was a strong supporter of the alliance with the Byzantine Empire and worked to improve relations with the Emperor Manuel I Komnenos. \n\nRaymond III's regency was marked by controversy and power struggles with other nobles. He was eventually replaced as regent by Guy of Lusignan who would later become King of Jerusalem. Raymond III died in 1187 without an heir and the County of Tripoli passed to his godson Raymond IV. Throughout his life Raymond III was known for his military prowess and his diplomatic skills. He played a significant role in the history of the Crusader states and his legacy continued to shape the politics of the region for centuries to come. Raymond III was a key figure in the events leading up to the Battle of Hattin and the fall of Jerusalem to Saladin.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0110650